1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,655
This gateway stands almost four thousand metres up in the Andes.

2
00:00:04,655 --> 00:00:09,382
It is carved from a single block of stone, heavier than ten elephants.

3
00:00:09,382 --> 00:00:13,366
And some people say that no human hands could have made it.

4
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:16,968
A lost civilisation older than the ice age.

5
00:00:16,968 --> 00:00:18,353
A city of the gods.

6
00:00:18,353 --> 00:00:21,488
A landing site for visitors from the stars.

7
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:23,966
You have probably heard all of it.

8
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:25,654
So tonight we dig.

9
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:34,266
Layer by layer, from the surface down to the bedrock of the claim, keeping a log of what is evidence, and what is only a story.

10
00:00:34,733 --> 00:00:37,022
Welcome to Tiwanaku, in Bolivia.

11
00:00:37,022 --> 00:00:43,030
Three thousand eight hundred and fifty metres above the sea, close to Lake Titicaca.

12
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:47,966
The air is thin, the winters are bitter, and nothing much grows here.

13
00:00:48,433 --> 00:00:50,093
Our question is simple.

14
00:00:50,093 --> 00:00:55,721
Is Tiwanaku the work of an advanced lost civilisation, or visitors from space?

15
00:00:55,721 --> 00:01:01,566
Or is the truth stranger, because it was made by people, with stone and patience?

16
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:06,682
Layer one.

17
00:01:06,682 --> 00:01:07,462
The surface.

18
00:01:07,462 --> 00:01:12,333
Let us see what is actually lying on the ground, before anyone explains it.

19
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:20,301
A stepped mound, called the Akapana, about sixteen metres tall and as wide as two football pitches.

20
00:01:20,301 --> 00:01:21,514
A sunken temple.

21
00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:24,848
A big walled platform called the Kalasasaya.

22
00:01:24,848 --> 00:01:26,666
And the Gate of the Sun.

23
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:33,200
The Spanish soldier Pedro Cieza de Leon wrote about the place in fifteen forty-nine.

24
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,266
The locals told him it was already ancient, and that nobody remembered who built it.

25
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:40,626
That matters.

26
00:01:40,626 --> 00:01:43,237
The people of Tiwanaku had no writing.

27
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:45,711
There is no inscription and no book.

28
00:01:45,711 --> 00:01:50,933
So everything we know has to come from the stones, the soil and the science.

29
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:56,543
Today it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, listed in the year two thousand.

30
00:01:56,543 --> 00:02:02,799
About four square kilometres of ruins, though what we see is only a part of what was here.

31
00:02:03,266 --> 00:02:03,976
Layer two.

32
00:02:03,976 --> 00:02:05,397
The Gate of the Sun.

33
00:02:05,397 --> 00:02:06,533
Our star object.

34
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,866
Almost three metres high, nearly four metres wide, cut from one piece of andesite, and weighing more than ten tonnes.

35
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:18,833
A doorway, with no door, and a crack running down one side.

36
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,446
Above the doorway is a carved figure, holding a staff in each hand.

37
00:02:24,446 --> 00:02:27,366
Archaeologists call him the Staff God.

38
00:02:27,366 --> 00:02:32,666
Some link him to Viracocha, a creator figure of later Andean stories.

39
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:38,461
Around him run rows of small winged attendants, some with the heads of birds.

40
00:02:38,461 --> 00:02:40,675
It is a crowded, ordered design.

41
00:02:40,675 --> 00:02:43,166
A lot of people see a message in it.

42
00:02:43,633 --> 00:02:45,315
Here is a first surprise.

43
00:02:45,315 --> 00:02:47,804
The Gate was not found standing here.

44
00:02:47,804 --> 00:02:48,679
It was moved.

45
00:02:48,679 --> 00:02:54,666
Early visitors saw it lying broken, and it was later set upright in the place we see now.

46
00:02:55,133 --> 00:03:02,805
So any claim based on exactly where the Gate points, or exactly how it lines up, begins on shaky ground.

47
00:03:02,805 --> 00:03:05,166
Its original place is not known.

48
00:03:05,633 --> 00:03:07,030
Scorecard note one.

49
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:11,220
A single block of andesite, carved and cracked: evidence.

50
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:14,160
Moved from its first position: evidence.

51
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:16,733
What the carvings mean: still open.

52
00:03:17,199 --> 00:03:18,083
Layer three.

53
00:03:18,083 --> 00:03:19,482
The calendar claim.

54
00:03:19,482 --> 00:03:28,833
And it begins with one man, an Austrian engineer named Arthur Posnansky, who came to Bolivia and stayed for almost forty years.

55
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:34,631
Between nineteen ten and nineteen forty-five Posnansky measured the site.

56
00:03:34,631 --> 00:03:40,766
He believed that its main platform was a giant observatory, built to follow the sun.

57
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:42,624
His idea was clever.

58
00:03:42,624 --> 00:03:47,702
The tilt of the Earth's axis changes very slowly over thousands of years.

59
00:03:47,702 --> 00:03:54,866
If the platform was lined up to an old angle, then the platform was built when the sky looked that way.

60
00:03:55,333 --> 00:04:01,364
He worked it out to an angle of twenty-three degrees, eight minutes, forty-eight seconds.

61
00:04:01,364 --> 00:04:07,666
And from that he concluded that Tiwanaku was between eleven and seventeen thousand years old.

62
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:09,832
Seventeen thousand years.

63
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:17,378
That would put it in the last ice age, before farming, before pottery, and long before any known city on Earth.

64
00:04:17,378 --> 00:04:20,233
If it were true, it would rewrite history.

65
00:04:20,699 --> 00:04:21,812
It is not true.

66
00:04:21,812 --> 00:04:36,500
When archaeologists dated charcoal and organic remains from the site, using radiocarbon, they found that Tiwanaku began around the year one hundred and ten, with an uncertainty of about sixty years.

67
00:04:36,966 --> 00:04:46,333
The big stone monuments came much later, from about the fourth to the sixth century, and the city peaked between five hundred and a thousand A D.

68
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,676
And the astronomical angle?

69
00:04:48,676 --> 00:04:56,323
Surveys have found no definite evidence that the Gate's carvings, or the platform, work as a precise calendar.

70
00:04:56,323 --> 00:04:58,199
The numbers do not hold up.

71
00:04:58,666 --> 00:05:00,359
There is a fair caveat.

72
00:05:00,359 --> 00:05:09,046
Some of the site does seem to line up with solstice and equinox sunrises, and the Akapana points at a sacred mountain.

73
00:05:09,046 --> 00:05:10,813
Farmers watched the sky.

74
00:05:10,813 --> 00:05:14,199
That is not a lost science, it is agriculture.

75
00:05:14,666 --> 00:05:16,259
Scorecard note two.

76
00:05:16,259 --> 00:05:21,792
Posnansky's date of fifteen thousand B C: debunked by radiocarbon.

77
00:05:21,792 --> 00:05:25,397
A calendar function for the Gate: unproven.

78
00:05:25,397 --> 00:05:28,666
Sky-watching by Andean farmers: likely.

79
00:05:29,133 --> 00:05:29,827
Layer four.

80
00:05:29,827 --> 00:05:30,457
The stone.

81
00:05:30,457 --> 00:05:37,899
If this is a task for the gods, we should look at what the stone was, where it came from, and what tools could cut it.

82
00:05:38,366 --> 00:05:46,357
The sandstone came from quarries on the Copacabana peninsula, about ninety kilometres of mountain and lake away.

83
00:05:46,357 --> 00:05:50,566
The harder andesite came from volcanic hills near the lake.

84
00:05:51,033 --> 00:05:52,290
Andesite is hard.

85
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:54,805
It is harder than most iron tools.

86
00:05:54,805 --> 00:06:01,980
But the builders had a plan, hammer stones of an even harder rock, sand as an abrasive, and time.

87
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:03,533
A great deal of time.

88
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:05,599
This is not guesswork.

89
00:06:05,599 --> 00:06:14,107
Two researchers, Jean-Pierre Protzen and Stella Nair, spent years cutting the same stone with the same kind of tools.

90
00:06:14,107 --> 00:06:18,833
Their book, The Stones of Tiahuanaco, shows exactly how it works.

91
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:22,371
Hammer, grind, rub, check the angle, repeat.

92
00:06:22,371 --> 00:06:23,138
It is slow.

93
00:06:23,138 --> 00:06:25,023
A skilled worker can do it.

94
00:06:25,023 --> 00:06:31,933
It leaves tool marks and chips, and it leaves a lot of broken stone, which is exactly what we find.

95
00:06:32,399 --> 00:06:33,364
And moving it?

96
00:06:33,364 --> 00:06:38,188
Ropes, ramps, wooden rollers, and hundreds of people pulling together.

97
00:06:38,188 --> 00:06:46,733
It is exactly how ancient builders moved stone all over the world, and Bolivian communities still move heavy loads this way.

98
00:06:47,199 --> 00:06:49,962
The Andes had a skill that matters here.

99
00:06:49,962 --> 00:06:50,722
Fibre rope.

100
00:06:50,722 --> 00:06:56,385
Their Inca successors built suspension bridges from grass that held for centuries.

101
00:06:56,385 --> 00:06:59,699
Rope is a technology, and they had plenty of it.

102
00:07:00,166 --> 00:07:05,603
So the stone is real, the tools are known, and the method has been tested.

103
00:07:05,603 --> 00:07:07,145
Scorecard note three.

104
00:07:07,145 --> 00:07:10,158
Cut by stone tools: evidence, replicated.

105
00:07:10,158 --> 00:07:12,582
Moved by rope and labour: likely.

106
00:07:12,582 --> 00:07:14,933
Needs a machine: no sign of one.

107
00:07:15,399 --> 00:07:16,245
Layer five.

108
00:07:16,245 --> 00:07:17,014
Pumapunku.

109
00:07:17,014 --> 00:07:21,628
This is the site that the ancient astronaut shows love best.

110
00:07:21,628 --> 00:07:24,166
The blocks look cut by a machine.

111
00:07:24,633 --> 00:07:27,178
Pumapunku means the gate of the puma.

112
00:07:27,178 --> 00:07:37,566
A platform about one hundred and sixty-seven metres by one hundred and seventeen, with the largest block weighing around one hundred and thirty tonnes.

113
00:07:38,033 --> 00:07:41,193
What gets attention are the H shaped blocks.

114
00:07:41,193 --> 00:07:46,867
Many are almost identical, with sharp edges, flat faces and neat inner corners.

115
00:07:46,867 --> 00:07:51,033
Online, people call it proof of a computer controlled saw.

116
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:52,861
Take that seriously.

117
00:07:52,861 --> 00:08:01,777
If a machine had cut these, we would expect identical copies, with the same dimensions on every block, and traces of machine tools.

118
00:08:01,777 --> 00:08:02,866
Do we find them?

119
00:08:03,333 --> 00:08:03,546
No.

120
00:08:03,546 --> 00:08:08,529
Researchers who measured the blocks found that they are not identical.

121
00:08:08,529 --> 00:08:13,655
Dimensions vary, edges differ and the work shows signs of hand dressing.

122
00:08:13,655 --> 00:08:15,933
There is no standardised module.

123
00:08:16,399 --> 00:08:18,187
Then came a clever test.

124
00:08:18,187 --> 00:08:29,133
In twenty eighteen, a team including the archaeologist Alexei Vranich made a 3D printed model to see how the blocks fit together, like a giant kit.

125
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:31,317
The result is a surprise.

126
00:08:31,317 --> 00:08:35,783
The pieces were meant to slot together, in a few repeating types.

127
00:08:35,783 --> 00:08:43,066
Not a mystery of lost machines, but a smart system for building a big platform from carved stone elements.

128
00:08:43,533 --> 00:08:46,502
Many blocks have small, I shaped sockets.

129
00:08:46,502 --> 00:08:52,367
They held metal clamps, poured in as molten copper alloy, to lock stone to stone.

130
00:08:52,367 --> 00:08:56,133
Pre Inca Andean smiths were skilled at exactly that.

131
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:00,059
And why does Pumapunku look like a pile of rubble?

132
00:09:00,059 --> 00:09:01,719
An earthquake, many say.

133
00:09:01,719 --> 00:09:03,656
But the real story is worse.

134
00:09:03,656 --> 00:09:11,266
After the Spanish arrived, the stones were looted, burned for lime, and used to build churches, and a railway.

135
00:09:11,733 --> 00:09:17,843
Whole walls at Tiwanaku went into the cathedral at La Paz and the nearby villages.

136
00:09:17,843 --> 00:09:21,793
Soldiers even used the monuments for target practice.

137
00:09:21,793 --> 00:09:25,966
What we see is the survivor of five centuries of damage.

138
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:33,803
Later, in the nineteen sixties and again recently, parts were rebuilt, sometimes badly, with cement and adobe.

139
00:09:33,803 --> 00:09:39,633
So some of the strange looks of the place come from restoration, not from the builders.

140
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:41,716
Scorecard note four.

141
00:09:41,716 --> 00:09:45,192
Pumapunku blocks very precise: partly true.

142
00:09:45,192 --> 00:09:47,294
Machine made: no evidence.

143
00:09:47,294 --> 00:09:49,638
Identical modules: not found.

144
00:09:49,638 --> 00:09:53,600
Interlocking system and metal clamps: documented.

145
00:09:54,066 --> 00:09:54,768
Layer six.

146
00:09:54,768 --> 00:09:55,679
The builders.

147
00:09:55,679 --> 00:09:57,363
If not aliens, then who?

148
00:09:57,363 --> 00:10:00,800
The answer is unglamorous, and rather impressive.

149
00:10:01,266 --> 00:10:08,994
They farmed raised fields, on ridges and ditches around Lake Titicaca, that kept crops alive through frost.

150
00:10:08,994 --> 00:10:13,399
They herded llamas, and traded across the Andes, in caravans.

151
00:10:13,866 --> 00:10:21,534
At its peak, between ten and forty thousand people lived in and around the city, according to different estimates.

152
00:10:21,534 --> 00:10:25,166
A capital, with temples, workshops and a ruling class.

153
00:10:25,633 --> 00:10:33,247
They left pottery, textiles, jewellery and ritual cups, with the same Staff God figure again and again.

154
00:10:33,247 --> 00:10:38,200
Pieces from Tiwanaku have been found as far away as Chile and Peru.

155
00:10:38,666 --> 00:10:40,242
And they left giants.

156
00:10:40,242 --> 00:10:50,373
The Bennett Monolith, about seven metres tall, is a carved stone figure that was moved to La Paz, and returned in two thousand and two.

157
00:10:50,373 --> 00:10:52,400
Its style is pure Tiwanaku.

158
00:10:52,866 --> 00:10:54,913
Even now, the picture grows.

159
00:10:54,913 --> 00:11:04,197
A two thousand sixteen drone survey mapped about four hundred hectares, and found new buildings, including a large temple area.

160
00:11:04,197 --> 00:11:07,633
Lidar scans also show buried structures nearby.

161
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:09,643
Scorecard note five.

162
00:11:09,643 --> 00:11:16,822
A native Andean state, named Tiwanaku, with farmers, artisans and rulers: strongly evidenced.

163
00:11:16,822 --> 00:11:22,766
Outside engineers or visitors: nothing in the ground, nothing in the pottery.

164
00:11:23,233 --> 00:11:24,074
Layer seven.

165
00:11:24,074 --> 00:11:25,476
The end of the city.

166
00:11:25,476 --> 00:11:32,066
Around the year one thousand, Tiwanaku fell, and the cause may be the saddest part of the log.

167
00:11:32,533 --> 00:11:35,861
Lake and soil records show long dry spells.

168
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:39,034
Raised fields failed, and food ran short.

169
00:11:39,034 --> 00:11:43,833
By about ten fifty, the great ceremonial centre was abandoned.

170
00:11:44,299 --> 00:11:49,108
No fire from the sky, no sudden departure, no ship in the clouds.

171
00:11:49,108 --> 00:11:51,846
A society that could not feed itself.

172
00:11:51,846 --> 00:11:56,433
It is a lesson that is a lot more modern than any alien story.

173
00:11:56,899 --> 00:11:59,056
Which brings us back to the Gate.

174
00:11:59,056 --> 00:12:01,277
A beautiful, moved, cracked block.

175
00:12:01,277 --> 00:12:03,433
And a question we can answer now.

176
00:12:03,899 --> 00:12:04,433
Debrief.

177
00:12:04,433 --> 00:12:07,366
Time to close the log, and score the claims.

178
00:12:07,366 --> 00:12:08,633
First the evidence.

179
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:11,215
Seven claims go on the board.

180
00:12:11,215 --> 00:12:15,738
Each one is scored as evidence, likely, unproven, or debunked.

181
00:12:15,738 --> 00:12:18,000
Here is the verdict on the dig.

182
00:12:18,466 --> 00:12:21,787
A gate carved by people, then moved: evidence.

183
00:12:21,787 --> 00:12:27,130
A city of tens of thousands, from about one hundred and ten A D: evidence.

184
00:12:27,130 --> 00:12:32,689
Stone cut by hand, with blocks locked by copper clamps, and tested: evidence.

185
00:12:32,689 --> 00:12:35,866
A drought collapse around ten fifty: likely.

186
00:12:36,333 --> 00:12:38,466
Machine cutting: no trace.

187
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:41,008
Fifteen thousand B C: debunked.

188
00:12:41,008 --> 00:12:44,700
Ancient astronauts: entirely unsubstantiated.

189
00:12:45,166 --> 00:12:46,066
The verdict.

190
00:12:46,066 --> 00:12:46,740
Grounded.

191
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:56,635
Tiwanaku was built by the Tiwanaku people, in the early centuries after Christ, with stone tools, rope, copper and organised labour.

192
00:12:56,635 --> 00:13:00,533
The Gate of the Sun is a masterpiece, not a mystery.

193
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,222
But there is still a puzzle that no one has solved.

194
00:13:04,222 --> 00:13:08,330
What did the carvings on the Gate mean to the people who made it?

195
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:11,299
The Staff God stares back, and has not told us.

196
00:13:11,766 --> 00:13:18,748
Next case: a wall of enormous stones, fitted so closely you cannot slide a blade between them.

197
00:13:18,748 --> 00:13:20,605
Inca, or something older?

198
00:13:20,605 --> 00:13:22,833
Next time on Ancient Machines.

199
00:13:23,299 --> 00:13:26,170
What do you think the Staff God was telling us?

200
00:13:26,170 --> 00:13:31,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
